# Untitled

Removal of Congo red dye from aqueous solution with nickel-based metal-organic framework/graphene oxide composites prepared by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling
College of Materials Science and Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha 410082, China
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling Metal-organic framework (MOF) Congo red Adsorption removal
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) were successfully synthesized by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling. In the absence of organic solvent, the coupling effect of ultrasonic wave and mechanical force played an significant role in the synthesis of MOFs. Adsorption of Congo red (CR) was studied in view of adsorption kinetic, isotherm and thermodynamics. The adsorbent was carried out using X-ray diffraction (XRD), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms, Raman spectroscopy and scanning electron microscope (SEM) methods. It was found that pseudo-second-order kinetic model and Freundlich adsorption isotherm matched well for the adsorption of CR onto nickel-based metal-organic framework/graphene oxide composites (Ni-MOF/GO). The results of the adsorption thermodynamics indicated that the adsorption process was a spontaneous and endothermic process. The adsorption capacity of graphene oxide/metal-organic frameworks (GO/MOFs) for CR reached \(2489\;\mathrm{mg/g}\), much higher than previous reports. It was demonstrated that an increase in the number of active metal sites can dramatically improve the adsorption capacity of dye. A suitable dry temperature is beneficial for the improvement of adsorption capacity for dye. In this paper, the adsorption results indicated that ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling has a good prospect for synthesis of MOFs with excellent adsorption performance.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, water pollution, especially organic dye wastewater, has attracted great attention with the development of industry. As is known to all, many dyes are considered to be toxic and even carcinogenic. In addition, some dyes are generally stable to light and oxidation [1], which makes them not easily to degrade.
Nowadays, there are many techniques for the separation and removal of organic dyes from wastewater, such as photocatalytic degradation [2], flocculation combined with flotation [3], membrane filtration [4], ion-exchange [5] and so on. Nevertheless, when dye wastewater is treated with these technologies, there are still some shortcomings that should be avoided, including expensive, the secondary pollution, undesirable removal efficiency to a small amount of dyes [6–8].
Adsorption technology, due to its high efficiency, low cost and feasibility, shows a great potential in the removal dyes from wastewater [9–11]. Over the past few years, many traditional adsorbents have been used in industrial dyed wastewater treatment, including activated carbon [12], zeolite [13], orange peel [14] and natural minerals [15].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a new class of crystalline porous materials based on metal ions connected by organic linkers, have drawn more and more attention in industry wastewater treatment due to their high porosity, large surface area and tunable pore size [10,16–19]. In the past few years, MOFs have been widely studied in various fields including catalysis [20,21], drug delivery [22], adsorption [17,23–25], separation [26,27], polymerization [28], electrode materials [29,30] and luminescence [31]. Among of these applications, adsorption is regarded as one of the most important applications of MOFs, which can be attributed to high porosity and huge surface area. However, the open framework and the large number of void spaces are not able to provide strong forces to retain small molecules in the adsorption process. Additionally, the stability of some MOFs is poor in humid conditions. To mitigate the disadvantages, some new technologies were reported, such as combining MOFs with other substrates [32–34], grafting with functional groups [35] and substituting organic linkers [36]. In this study, graphene oxide was used to prepare composites with MOFs. The concept of MOF/GO nanocomposites have been reported by Bandosz's group [37–39]. It is generally considered that new pore space will be created at the interface between MOFs blocks
and GO, which can increase the dispersive forces and can effectively retain the guest molecules [37,39]. In fact, there have been many MOF/ GO composites successfully applied in the removal of organic pollutants, such as In-MOF/GO [34], HKUST1/GO [40] and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{HKUST1}/\) GO [41]. Therefore, MOF/GO nanocomposites can be expected to be excellent adsorbents for dye wastewater treatment.
Generally, hydrothermal or solvothermal method, as a traditional method, has been used to synthesized MOFs for a long time. However, it requires long reaction time and a large amount of organic solvent [42]. To get a more convenient and environmentally synthesis process, it is necessary to find other methods for MOFs synthesis. In recent years, alternative techniques, such as ultrasound-assisted method and microwave-assisted method, have been applied to synthesize some MOFs [43,44]. For example, the ultrasound-assisted synthesis of amine-functionalized \(Z\mathfrak{n}(\mathrm{II})\) -based MOF was reported in Ahmad Afzalinia group [45]. A Morsali et al. successfully prepared \(Z\mathfrak{n}(\mathrm{II})\) -based metal-organic frameworks (TMU-5) by ultrasound assisted method [46]. The above method for the synthesis of MOFs can not only decrease reaction time from several days to a couple of hours even minutes, but also avoid the use of a large amount of solvent [42].
In this paper, to the best of our knowledge, solid-liquid reaction ultrasound-assisted ball milling [47–49] is firstly reported by our research group to prepare Ni-MOF/GO at low temperature \((\leq100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . Compared with hydrothermal or solvothermal methods, ultrasound assisted technology is a faster, more economical and greener approach to synthesis MOFs [50–53]. In the absence of organic solvent, our group chose ultrasound assisted approach as an effective method to make the raw materials easily soluble in liquid medium and the coupling effect of ultrasonic wave and mechanical force played an significant role in the synthesis of MOFs [54,55]. And it is believed that acoustic cavitation created by high-intensity ultrasonic irradiation can greatly accelerated the chemical reaction [45,46,56,57]. Moreover, the MOFs prepared by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling showed excellent adsorption performance. Therefore, this technique can be expected to be a potential approach for synthesis of MOFs.
Herein, a simple and efficient route to prepare GO/MOFs was shown. In this paper, the adsorption of CR onto Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs was investigated in respect of adsorption kinetics, isotherms and thermodynamics to determine the superior adsorbent. Additionally, the adsorption of CR over GO/MOFs dried at different temperatures and the effect of pH was also studied in detail.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
1,3,5-Benzentricarboxylic acid (trimesic acid, \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) , \(98\%\) ) and nickel acetate \((\geq99.0\%)\) were purchased from Aladdin biological technology Co., LTD, Shanghai, China. Congo red (CR) was purchased from Aladdin biological technology Co., LTD, Shanghai, China. All the materials in this study were used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni-MOFs
Nickel acetate \(\left(4.44\:\mathrm{g}\right.\) , \(17.84\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , 1,3,5-trimesic acid \(\left(2.5\;\mathrm{g}\right.\) , \(11.9\,\mathrm{mmol})\) , stainless steel ball \(\left(560\;\mathrm{g}\right)\) and deionized water \(\left(700\,\mathrm{ml}\right)\) were put into a solid-liquid reaction ball milling device assisted by ultrasonic wave. The rotation speed of stirrer was \(235\;\mathrm{rpm}\) , and the ultrasonic intensity and power were \(40\,\mathrm{kHz}\) and \(200\;\mathrm{W}\) . The reaction lasted for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and then the product was dried for \(^{6\,\mathrm{h}}\) .
## 2.3. Synthesis of Ni-MOF/ GO composites
A certain amount of GO was added into the dissolved Ni \(\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}/H_{3}B T C}\) mixtures. Then resulting suspensions subjected to the same steps as the synthesis of Ni-MOFs.
## 2.4. Adsorption measurements
An aqueous solution of CR was prepared by dissolving CR in deionized water. To determine the CR concentrations, the absorbance values of the solutions were analyzed by ultraviolet spectrometer (UV-2550 220 V Shimadzu instruments (Suzhou) Co., Ltd.) at \(495.5\;\mathrm{nm}\) . And the calibration curve was obtained from the spectra of the standard solutions \((0{-}50\,\mathrm{ppm})\) .
Generally, the adsorbents would be dried in a vacuum oven before adsorption. And \(25\;\mathrm{mg}\) adsorbents were put into the CR solution \(\left(100\;\mathrm{ml}\right)\) with different concentrations \((50{-}700\;\mathrm{ppm})\) . Then the solution was stirred for a fixed time \((10\,\mathrm{min}{-}12\,\mathrm{h})\) on a magnetic stirrer. And after adsorption, the solution was separated from the adsorbents through a syringe filter (PTFE, hydrophilic, \(0.45\;\upmu\mathrm{m})\) , and the solution concentration was calculated with an absorbance obtained from the UV spectra, after dilution if necessary. The adsorption amount of CR is calculated by the following equation:
\[
\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}=(\mathbf{c}_{0}-\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{t}})\frac{\mathbf{V}}{\mathbf{m}}
\]
where \({\bf C}_{0}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{t}}\) \(\mathrm{(mg/L)}\) are the concentrations of MB solutions at time \(=0\) and t respectively. v (L) is the volume of the MB solution and \(\mathbf{m}\) (g) is the weight of the adsorbents.
## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Characterization results
The XRD patterns of the materials studied in this work are shown in Fig. 1(a). The peak of GO at 2θ is about \(11.42^{\circ}\) , representing the interlayer distance of GO is \(7.7\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) . In the patterns, the diffraction patterns of GO/MOFs are basically consistent with that of pure materials, suggesting that the addition of GO didn't disturb the formation of MOFs and the composites preserved the structure of Ni-MOFs. Fig. 1(b) showed the thermogravimetric curves of Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs. As shown in Fig. 1(b), there exists two main stages of thermal decomposition for the materials with increasing temperature. A slight weight loss at temperature lower than \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is corresponded to the removal of physically adsorbed water. The second stage is attributed to the collapse of the structures for the samples, indicating that the samples can be stable up to \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
Fig. 2(a) showed the Nitrogen adsorption and desorption isotherms of Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs. According to the IUPAC classification, the isotherms measured on Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs belong to type-II sorption behavior with small hysteresis [58]. Fig. 2(b) showed the pore size distribution curves of Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs. It can be seen from Fig. 2(a) and (b) that Ni-MOFs and the composites are mesoporous \((2{-}50\,\mathrm{nm})\) metal-organic frameworks. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs are \(59.8\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(69.6\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) , respectively. The enhanced surface area may be attributed to the new pores created at the interface between MOF units and GO [34].
To further confirm the existence of GO in the composites, the Raman spectra of GO, Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs was shown in Fig. 3. It can be seen that two obvious peaks at 1322 and \(1583\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponding to the D and G bands were observed in the spectrum of GO [59]. Furthermore, the Raman spectra of GO/MOFs showed bands corresponding to Ni-MOFs as well as the D and G bands. These results verify the effective combination between GO and Ni-MOFs.
Fig. 4(a), (c) and (d) showed the SEM images of Ni-MOFs synthesized by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling, ultrasonic wave and ball-milling, respectively. As shown in Fig. 1(a), the diffraction patterns of Ni-MOFs-2 and Ni-MOFs-3 are basically consistent with Ni-MOFs-1. Fig. 4(b) displayed the SEM image of GO/MOFs synthesized by ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling. It can be seen that Fig. 4(a) showed large amount of finer cuboids compared with Fig. 4(c), indicating that
ball-milling can effectively suppress the grain growth and refine crystalline particles. The effect of ultrasonic irradiation on morphology of Ni-MOFs was investigated between Fig. 4(a) and (d). Smaller particles were observed in Fig. 4(d). However, Ni-MOFs synthesized by ball-milling does not show regular morphology in the absence of ultrasonic wave. Generally, ultrasonic wave can promote better growth and simultaneously prevent the agglomeration of particles [47], leading to forming regular and independent cuboids. As shown in Fig. 4(b), some wrinkles were observed in accordance with the characteristic of GO [59,60] and the Ni-MOFs were embedded in the GO layers, suggesting that Ni-MOFs were well decorated with GO.
## 3.2. Adsorption kinetics
In this paper, pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetics models [61–63] were used to test the experimental data and analyzed the adsorption kinetics.
Pseudo-first-order kinetic model:
\[
\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}}{\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{t}}}=\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{l}}(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{l}})
\]
\[
\ln(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{l}})=\ln\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{l}}\mathbf{t}
\]
Pseudo-second-order kinetic model:
\[
\frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}}{\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{t}}}=\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{l}}(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}})^{2}
\]
\[
\frac{\mathrm{t}}{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{t}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{k}_2\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{e}}^2}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{e}}}\mathrm{t}
\]
The adsorption kinetics describe the change of adsorption amount
with time. Where \(\mathbf{q_{e}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)\) is the equilibrium adsorption amount determined from the reciprocal of the slopes of the plots in Fig. 5, \(\mathbf{q_{t}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{mg/}\right)\) g) is the amount adsorbed at time t, t (min) is adsorption time, \(\mathrm{K}_{1}\) \((\mathrm{min}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\) \(\mathrm{{(g/(mg\cdotmin))}}\) are the rate constant of pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetics respectively.
Fig. 5 showed the plot of pseudo-second-order kinetic of CR over NiMOFs and GO/MOFs. The adsorption kinetic constants (K) and correlation coefficients \((\mathbf{R}^{2})\) calculated from the pseudo-first-order and
pseudo-second-order kinetic models were shown in Table 1. It can be seen from Fig. 5 that the adsorption amount of GO/MOFs is higher than that of Ni-MOFs at \(\mathrm{C_{0}}=50\;\mathrm{ppm}\) . And the calculated adsorption kinetics constant K of GO/MOFs is larger than that of Ni-MOFs. Additionally, as shown in Table 1, the correlation coefficients \((\mathbf{R}^{2})\) obtained from the pseudo-second-order kinetic model are higher \((\mathbf{R}^{2}\,>\,0.99)\) than that of the pseudo-first-order kinetic model and the \(\mathbf{q_{e}}\) values calculated with pseudo-second-order model are very close to the experimental data, indicating that pseudo-second-order kinetic model matched well with the adsorption process. Moreover, the rate constants \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\) increased with temperature, which suggested that the initial adsorption rate increased with temperature.
## 3.3. Adsorption isotherms
In order to describe the adsorption isotherm more scientifically, there are Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models used to analyze the relationship between the adsorption amount of CR and its equilibrium concentration in aqueous solution.
Langmuir adsorption isotherm model [61,62] assumes that adsorption is monomolecular and occurs on a homogeneous surface with all the adsorption sites possessing affinities for the adsorbate [25]. The equation is in the following form:
\[
\frac{\mathsf{c_e}}{\mathsf{q_e}}=\frac{\mathsf{c_e}}{\mathsf{Q_0}}+\frac{1}{\mathsf{Q_0b}}
\]
where \(\mathrm{C_{e}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{mg/L}\right)\) is the equilibrium concentration of CR solution, \(\mathbf{q_{e}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)\) is the equilibrium adsorption amount, \(\mathrm{Q_{0}}\) \(\left(\mathrm{mg/g}\right)\) is the Langmuir constant (maximum adsorption capacity), b \(\mathrm{L/mg}\) or \(\mathrm{L/mol}\) is
the Langmuir constant related to the free energy of adsorption.
The Freundlich adsorption isotherm model [23] is used to describe a heterogeneous adsorption surfaces with multilayer adsorption [25]. The equation is in the following form:
\[
\ln q_{e}=\ln k_{f}+\frac{1}{n}\ln c_{e}
\]
where \(\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{f}}\left(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g}\right)\) and \(\mathbf{n}\) are the Freundlich constants related to the adsorption capacity and intensity respectively. These constants can be calculated from the intercept and slope of linear plots of \(\ln{\bf Q}_{\mathrm{e}}\) versus \(\ln{\bf C}_{\mathrm{e}}\) in Fig. 6(b).
The adsorption isotherms were obtained after adsorption for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) .
Adsorption isotherms of CR onto Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs were shown in Fig. 6(a). It can be clearly seen that the adsorption amount of GO/ MOFs is higher than that of Ni-MOFs, which can be attributed to the addition of GO. As shown in Fig. 7, the adsorption capacity increased with adsorption temperature, suggesting that the adsorption process is endothermic. Table 2 listed the Langmuir and Freundlich isotherms parameters for CR adsorption over GO/MOFs. The adsorption of CR onto GO/MOFs matched quite well \((\mathrm{R}^{2}\ >\ 0.99)\) with the Freundlich model compared with the Langmuir model at different temperatures and all the \(1/{\mathfrak{n}}\) values are less than 1(0.5176–0.6719) in the adsorption temperatures, which suggests that GO/MOFs possesses heterogeneous adsorption surfaces and the adsorption of CR over GO/MOFs is multilayer adsorption [25].
Additionally, the adsorption capacities of CR over GO/MOFs were compared with those of other absorbents as shown in Table 3. The adsorption amount of GO/MOFs was calculated at certain initial concentrations. As can be seen from the Table 3, the adsorption capacity of GO/MOFs is much greater than that of other adsorbent materials
reported in the literature, which may be attributed to the addition of GO and more active metal sites.
## 3.4. Thermodynamic of adsorption
To get further insight into the mechanism in the process of CR adsorption, the thermodynamic equilibrium constant \(\left(\mathrm{K}_{0}\right)\) and Gibbs free energy change \(\left(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}\right)\) were determined on the following equation:
\[
K_{0}=\frac{q_{e}}{c_{e}}
\]
\[
\Delta G^{\circ}=-RT\ln K_{0}
\]
The enthalpy change \(\left(\Delta\mathbf{H}^{\circ}\right)\) and entropy change \(\left(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ}\right)\) were calculated from the Van't Hoff equation:
\[
\ln K_0=\frac{\Delta S^\circ}{R}-\frac{\Delta H^\circ}{RT}
\]
where \(\mathrm{K}_{0}\) is the Langmuir adsorption constant \(\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mol}\right)\), R is the gas constant \((8.314\;\mathrm{J}/\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{K})\). A linear plot of \(\ln\!\mathrm{K}_{0}\) versus \(1/\mathrm{T}\) was obtained as shown in Fig. 8. \(\Delta\mathbf{H}^{\circ}\) and \(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ}\) were calculated from the \((-{\mathsf{s l o p e}}\times\mathbf{R})\) and (intercept \(\times\textbf{R}\) ) of the Van't Hoff plot as shown in Table 4.
As shown in Table 4, the negative \(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}\) and positive \(\Delta\mathbf{H}^{\circ}\) indicated that the adsorption process of CR over GO/MOFs is spontaneous and endothermic. The enthalpy change because of chemisorption takes value between 84 and \(420\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\), and it is typically physical adsorption type bonds at the range of \(<84\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) [8]. Thus, the adsorption of CR onto GO/MOFs may be physisorption. Moreover, the entropy change \(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\circ}\) is positive, which revealed that the process resulted in an increase in randomness because the number of desorbed water molecules is larger than that of the adsorbed CR molecules (CR molecule is bigger than water molecule; therefore, several water molecules may be desorbed by adsorption of CR molecules) [17,64,65].
## 3.5. Effect of dry temperature on the adsorption of CR
To understand the role of coordinated water molecules on the adsorption capacity of CR, the amount of adsorbed CR over GO/MOFs dried at different temperatures was displayed in Fig. 9(a). As shown in Fig. 9(a), the adsorption amount of GO/MOFs dried at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is more than that of GO/MOFs dried at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Due to the water solvent, there will be more water molecules taking part in the coordination with metal sites in the formation process of GO/MOFs. And these water molecules can be removed from the framework after vacuum dry at \(423\,\mathrm{K}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\), which can provide more coordinative unsaturated metal sites as Lewis acid sites [35]. As shown in Fig. 9(b), it is interesting to find that the adsorption amount of cationic dye (MB) increased with decreasing pH (in the range of \(\mathrm{pH}\,<\,7\)). One possible reason is that there are more \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) sites as Lewis acid to interact with \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Lewis base) in the low pH solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,<\,7)\). Thus, a suitable dry temperature can improve the adsorption capacity of CR over GO/MOFs due to the acid-base
interaction between active metal sites (Lewis acid) and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Lewis base).
## 3.6. Effect of pH on the adsorption of CR
To determine the effect of pH on the adsorption capacity of CR at various pH values, the pH of CR solution was adjusted with \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{NaOH}\) or \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{HCl}\) aqueous solution. And this work was carried out at \(298\,\mathrm{k}\) and \(200\;\mathrm{ppm}\). CR is a kind of anionic form dye at basic pH and exists as cationic form at acid pH [66]. It was reported that the color of CR solution changed from red to dark blue with decreasing the pH. As shown in Fig. 10, the adsorption amount decreased with increasing the pH, which is similar to previously reported results [66–68]. It was mentioned that CR exists as anionic form at basic pH, and the concentration of negative surface charge of the adsorbent is increased with increasing the pH. Thus, the adsorption capacity of CR is low attributed to the ionic repulsion between the negative charged surface of GO/MOFs and anionic dye (CR) at basic pH. However, the amount of adsorbed CR is much greater at acidic pH due to the presence of functional groups like carboxylic (–COOH) and hydroxyl (–OH) provided by GO. Cationic dye molecules at acidic pH can get attached on the surface of GO/MOFs by replacing \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)ion of carboxylic and hydroxyl, which resulted in higher adsorption amount at acidic environments [66]. Additionally, it was also mentioned that there may be more \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) sites as Lewis acid to improve the adsorption amount due to acid-base interaction.
## 4. Conclusion
In this work, ultrasonic wave-assisted ball milling is firstly reported by our laboratory to prepare metal-organic frameworks. In the absence of organic solvent, the chemical reaction can be also promoted because of the coupling effect of ultrasonic wave and mechanical force. CR adsorption over Ni-MOFs and GO/MOFs from aqueous solution was studied in view of the adsorption kinetic, adsorption isotherm, thermodynamics, dry temperatures and the effect of pH. The results indicate that the pseudo-second-order kinetic model matches quite well with the adsorption of CR over GO/MOFs. The Freundlich model fits the adsorption isotherm better than the Langmuir model. The negative \(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{\circ}\) and positive \(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}\) suggest that the adsorption process is spontaneous and endothermic. Compared with other adsorbents, the GO/MOFs shows higher adsorption capacity up to \(2489\;\mathrm{mg/g}\). It is found that increasing the number of active metal sites is an effective method to improve the adsorption capacity of dye. These results show that ultrasound-assisted synthesis is an effective approach for fast, environment-friendly and economical preparation of MOFs.
